From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Attard <sattard@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 20:15:53 +0000
Subject: fix: plumb the requesting frame through pointer lock requests

Blink sends Element.requestPointerLock() to the browser over the
WidgetInputHandlerHost of the requesting frame's local root, so the
browser only learns which widget asked. Frames that share a process with
their parent (same-site cross-origin iframes under site-per-process, or
any iframe without site isolation) share that widget, which makes the
request indistinguishable from one made by the local root document.

Electron reports the requesting frame and origin to the app's permission
request handler, so it needs the real frame. This passes the requesting
frame's LocalFrameToken with the request, validates in WebContentsImpl
that it names a live frame in the widget's process whose local root is
that widget, records it next to pointer_lock_widget_ and exposes it via
WebContentsImpl::GetPointerLockRequestingFrame().

Chrome keys pointer lock on the top-level tab so it has not needed this;
it could be proposed upstream alongside crbug.com/492211919 (the sandbox
flag check has the same local-root-only limitation).

